\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Our model correctly disentangles between student aptitude and school characteristics, whereas the GVAE fails. We ask the counterfactual question ``What score would student $k$ from school $A$ have obtained if they had attended the \textit  {typical school}?''. The task is to generate a set of test scores by translating the scores from school $A$ onto the density of scores from the typical school. Each line shows the translation for an individual student. Our model preserves the relative positions of the scores, whilst also capturing the density of the typical school.}}{11}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trans}{{1.3}{11}{Our model correctly disentangles between student aptitude and school characteristics, whereas the GVAE fails. We ask the counterfactual question ``What score would student $k$ from school $A$ have obtained if they had attended the \textit {typical school}?''. The task is to generate a set of test scores by translating the scores from school $A$ onto the density of scores from the typical school. Each line shows the translation for an individual student. Our model preserves the relative positions of the scores, whilst also capturing the density of the typical school}{figure.caption.10}{}}
